<section>
  <h2>How Each Claim Was Established</h2>

  <p class="lede">Four defects in this feature's development reached a fully green test suite
  before being caught by running the built artifact. That history sets the standard for what
  counts as evidence here, and it is higher than "the tests pass".</p>

  <h3>The navigation fix: a matched pair</h3>

  <p>The strongest evidence produced in this work was accidental. An end-to-end test written
  for the fix was first run against a stale build that predated it — and failed, reporting that
  the page had navigated to the storage URL. The defect, reproducing verbatim, against the test
  meant to catch it.</p>

  <p>That was then done deliberately: the pre-fix module restored from version control, the
  matrix run, the fix restored, the matrix run again — identical lanes, one variable changed.</p>

  <table class="runs">
    <thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Lanes passing</th><th>Navigation events</th><th>Spec executions</th></tr></thead>
    <tr><td>Pre-fix</td><td>0 of 3</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-fix</td><td>3 of 3</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>Three engines, all in one container image so that no engine is special-cased. The failure
  appears and disappears with the one thing that changed. That is a stronger claim than any
  number of passing runs, because a passing run is consistent with the test not working at
  all.</p>

  {{ c.warn('What no unit test can prove here', 'The defect is top-frame navigation, and the browser emulator used for component tests does not implement navigation. It prints a note and continues. No unit or component test can reproduce this class of defect; the component test for the frame says so in its own header, so nobody later mistakes a green run for proof.') }}

  <h3>The structural guard that was tested by breaking it</h3>

  <p>The archived-object check depends on the provider being recorded on the job. If that
  recording is ever removed, nothing throws — the check simply flags nothing, and archived
  objects are queued again exactly as before. Silent degradation.</p>

  <p>A structural assertion now guards it. Rather than trusting a test written after the code,
  the field was temporarily removed to confirm the guard failed, then restored. A test that has
  never been seen to fail is not yet known to test anything.</p>

  <h3>Where verification stops short</h3>

  <p>The folder check reports four outcomes rather than two, because two would require claiming
  more than is known.</p>

  <table class="matrix">
    <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Basis</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>
    <tr><td class="verdict">Confirmed</td><td>Name and size both match, and the name is claimed
        by exactly one file in the job.</td><td>Promoted to done.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="verdict">Missing</td><td>Absent, with no collision-renamed variant
        present.</td><td>Marked failed. <em>(Correction 2026-08-01: the intended resume
        never happens — the job's status stays DONE and the resume list excludes DONE, so
        the retry this row promised is unreachable. Postmortem finding F3; fixed in the
        lifecycle redesign.)</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="verdict">Wrong size</td><td>Present under the right name at the wrong
        length.</td><td>Marked failed. <em>(Same F3 correction as above.)</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="verdict">Cannot tell</td><td>Two files in the job share a local name, or a
        collision-renamed variant exists.</td><td><strong>Left alone.</strong> Not confirmed,
        and deliberately not retried.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>The last row is the load-bearing one. Treating "cannot tell" as "missing" would re-download
  files already sitting on disk, at the user's expense, to fix a problem that does not exist.</p>

  <h3>Coverage</h3>

  <table class="runs">
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Count</th><th>What it can and cannot show</th></tr></thead>
    <tr><td>Unit</td><td>1,318</td><td>Logic in isolation. Cannot execute the bundle.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Component</td><td>457</td><td>Rendering and interaction. Cannot navigate.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>End-to-end</td><td>9 browser lanes</td><td>The built bundle in real engines against
        a live mock service. The only layer where the navigation defect is observable.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p><em>Correction 2026-08-01: the end-to-end row must not be read as covering the two
  v1.44.0 features. Archived-object flagging and folder verification had no browser
  coverage of any kind — the mock cannot represent storage classes and the picker was never
  stubbed — and the postmortem further showed the e2e environment could not observe any
  download at all (its transport is http, which the bundle's CSP blocks for the download
  frame). Coverage claims in this table are superseded by the postmortem.</em></p>
</section>
